.TH th_read 3 "November 2025" "University of Illinois" "C Library Calls"
.SH NAME
th_read, th_write \- read and write a file header block from a tar archive
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <libtar.h>
.P
.BI "int th_read(TAR *" t ");"

.BI "int th_write(TAR *" t ");"
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.BR th_read ()
function reads the next logical tar record from the archive associated
with the
.I TAR
handle
.IR t .
It sets the current tar header associated with
.I t
to the contents of the terminal ordinary header block of that record.

A logical record consists of zero or more GNU extension headers
(\fBL\fR for a long name and \fBK\fR for a long link target), each
followed by the complete number of 512-byte data blocks declared by its
12-byte size field, followed by the ordinary header.  The extensions may
appear in any order and may repeat; the last copy of each kind overrides
earlier copies.  An \fBL\fR extension sets
.IR th_buf.gnu_longname ,
which
.BR th_get_pathname ()
returns, and a \fBK\fR extension sets
.IR th_buf.gnu_longlink ,
which
.BR th_get_linkname ()
returns.  Overrides apply only to the record immediately following the
extension chain; the next
.BR th_read ()
starts with no inherited extension state.

The GNU extension size field is decoded with a strict bounded grammar:
leading and trailing spaces or NUL bytes are allowed around one or more
octal digits, but padding is not allowed inside the digit run; signs,
8/9 digits, other non-octal characters, base-256 values and a zero
length are rejected.  Only the 12 size bytes are inspected.  Declared
payload bytes are normalized into library-owned, NUL-terminated strings:
either exactly \fIn\fR non-NUL bytes, or \fIn\-1\fR non-NUL bytes plus a
NUL in the final declared byte.  An earlier NUL is an error, and block
padding past the declared \fIn\fR bytes is never scanned.

Each handle created by
.BR tar_open ()
or
.BR tar_fdopen ()
starts with a per-logical-record budget of 1 MiB of declared extension
payload bytes and 64 extension headers.  These limits can be changed
with
.BR tar_set_gnu_extension_limits ().
Every declaration counts towards the byte total, including copies later
overridden, and every extension header counts towards the header total.
Equalling a limit is allowed; exceeding either limit fails before the
offending payload is allocated or read.  Budgets reset at the start of
the next
.BR th_read ()
and are independent per
.I TAR
handle.

.BR th_read ()
does not consume regular-file body data; the caller advances past file
bodies with
.BR tar_skip_regfile ()
or the extraction routines.

The
.BR th_write ()
function writes the contents of the current header associated with
.I t
to the tar archive associated with
.IR t .
.SH "OBJECT LIFETIME"
The strings returned by
.BR th_get_pathname ()
and
.BR th_get_linkname (),
and the
.IR th_buf.gnu_longname / th_buf.gnu_longlink
pointers, are owned by the
.I TAR
handle.  The entry view they belong to is invalidated at the
.B START
of the next
.BR th_read ()
call, which releases the previous GNU extension strings and clears
.IR th_buf .
They are also released by
.BR tar_close ().
Callers must copy a name that must survive the next
.BR th_read ()
or
.BR tar_close ().
.SH "RETURN VALUE"
On successful completion of a complete, validated logical record,
.BR th_read ()
returns 0.  On a clean end of archive it returns 1.  On failure it
returns \-1 and sets
.IR errno .

After any failure, the handle enters a sticky read-error state: further
.BR th_read ()
calls return \-1 with the original
.I errno
and invoke no reader function, and
.BR tar_set_gnu_extension_limits ()
does not clear the state.  The only productive remaining operation is
.BR tar_close (),
which releases all memory still owned by the handle and calls the
backend close function.  On a failed read,
.I th_buf.gnu_longname
and
.I th_buf.gnu_longlink
are NULL.
.SH ERRORS
.BR th_read ()
and
.BR th_write ()
will fail if:
.IP \fBEINVAL\fP
A short header or payload read occurred, the extension size field or
content was malformed, a header failed magic/version/checksum
validation, or an extension chain was left dangling by EOF, terminator
blocks or a truncated following header.
.IP \fBENOMEM\fP
Memory for a normalized extension string could not be allocated.
.IP \fBE2BIG\fP
The per-record byte or extension-header limit was exceeded.
.PP
If the backend reader returns \-1, its
.I errno
value is preserved.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR tar_block_read (3),
.BR tar_set_gnu_extension_limits (3),
.BR tar_open (3),
.BR th_get_pathname (3),
.BR tar_close (3)
